# Line endings.
#
# This repository is authored on Windows and run on Linux. Without this file
# the two disagree about how a line ends, and the disagreement is invisible
# until it is expensive:
#
#   * A shell script committed with CRLF fails on the VM with the useless
#     message "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M". The caret-M is the
#     carriage return being read as part of the interpreter's name.
#   * A Python node with CRLF usually runs, so the problem hides until
#     something parses the file itself.
#   * arc-setup runs dos2unix to repair the first case, which rewrites the
#     file, which makes git report a modification, which blocks the next pull.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` tells git to store every text file with LF and to check it
# out with LF on every platform, Windows included. Modern editors on Windows,
# including VS Code and Notepad, handle LF without complaint, so there is no
# cost on that side and the ambiguity disappears from both.
#
# After adding this file, normalise what is already committed, once:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git commit -m "Normalise line endings to LF"
#
# That commit will look enormous and change no code. It is rewriting the stored
# line endings of every text file, which is exactly what it should do.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that must be preserved byte for byte. Running line-ending conversion
# over any of these corrupts them silently, and a corrupt mesh or map is a
# confusing thing to debug because the error surfaces far from the cause.

*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.ico     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.zip     binary
*.gz      binary
*.pgm     binary
*.pbm     binary
*.bag     binary
*.db3     binary
*.mcap    binary
*.stl     binary
*.dae     binary
*.mp4     binary
*.npy     binary
*.npz     binary
*.ttf     binary
*.woff    binary
*.woff2   binary
